Title: TEMPEST GENERATIONS
Credit: Written by
Author: Screenwriting Project
Draft date: November 1966 Deluge Draft
Contact: tempest-generations

===

EXT. PIAZZA DEL DUOMO, FLORENCE - NIGHT (NOV 4, 1966)

The cathedral dome looms against an asphalt sky, bruised violet by distant lightning.

Rain does not fall here. It drives. A shrieking SIROCCO gale rips through the Piazza at seventy miles an hour, twisting rain into white horizontal sheets.

A single 1958 Fiat 500 sloshes through two feet of foaming brown water, its weak yellow headlights cutting through the deluge.

INT. FIAT 500 - CONTINUOUS

ANTHONY FONTANA (32) grips the shuddering steering wheel. Queens accent, soaked trench coat, five-o'clock shadow. Next to him on the passenger seat sits a beat-up leather legal binder stamped: *FONTANA & SONS ARTISAN LEATHER - EST. 1892*.

ANTHONY
(muttering, wipers thrashing uselessly)
Two years in Vietnam and I'm gonna drown in the birthplace of the Renaissance.

The dashboard RADIO crackles with frantic Italian:

RADIO ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
*(in Italian, rapid, distorted)*
...il livello dell'Arno ha superato gli argini agli Uffizi... via dei Neri è completamente sommersa... chiunque si trovi ai piani bassi salga immediatamente...

Anthony smacks the dash. The radio wheezes into static.

EXT. VIA MAGGIO / SAN FREDIANO - MOMENTS LATER

The Arno has ceased to be a river; it is a moving ocean.

Down the narrow canyon of medieval stone palazzi, a THREE-METER WALL OF BLACK WATER charges at thirty miles an hour, carrying wooden market stalls, crushed Lambrettas, and floating wine barrels.

A violent POP echoes down the street as underground diesel fuel tanks burst under pressure. A black iridescent slick coats the rising surge.

INT. FONTANA BOTTEGA - GROUND FLOOR - CONTINUOUS

Water boils up through the flagstone floor drain like a geyser.

RENZO FONTANA (68), hands stained with decades of chestnut tannin and saddle oil, desperately stacks antique brass bookbinding dies onto high oak shelves.

The bottom shelf gives way under rising water. Centuries of hand-carved boxwood stamps crash into the murky slurry.

RENZO
(roaring in grief)
No! Maria Vergine, no!

The heavy oak front door GROANS under tremendous hydraulic pressure.

THROUGH THE GLASS TRANSOM:

Headlights sweep across the rushing black water outside. Anthony's Fiat rams against the bottega's stone lintel, half-submerged.

Anthony's face appears at the high iron grate, shouting against the roar of sixty thousand tons of water.

ANTHONY
Pop! Grab my hand! Climb!
